WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:01.901
-Stephen Merchant!

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.167
One of my faves.

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:04.634
Thanks for coming back
to the show.

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.434
-I love you, brother.
I love being on the show.

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:08.133
-It is Nana Week here
at the show

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.567
and I was talking
to you backstage

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:12.467
and I was saying do you have any
fond memory of your grandma?

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.267
-My grandmother --
a lovely woman,

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:15.868
a dear, sweet woman, Irene,

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:17.934
but not an optimistic lady.

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:19.667
[ Laughter ]
You know how there's like

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.167
there's people who are kind of
glass half full

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.067
and there are some
who are glass half empty.

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:26.667
Irene -- there's nothing
in the glass.

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:28.300
[ Laughter ]
The glass has been knocked

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.934
off the table, smashed,
and thrown in the trash.

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:32.434
-Interesting.

00:00:32.434 --> 00:00:34.467
-Like I remember,
when I was like 13,

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.834
she said to me, "What would you
like for Christmas?

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:36.934
What gift would you like?"
I said,

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:38.767
"I would love an overcoat
like Granddad."

00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.334
My granddad had this lovely
overcoat with this big,

00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:42.267
kind of, sort of
sheepskin collar.

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.200
I was like, "Can I get one
like that?"

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.000
And she said,
"Well, you can have his."

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.434
[ Laughter ]
And I said, "Well,

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:50.534
doesn't Granddad need it?"
And she said,

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:52.367
"He'll probably be dead soon."

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:55.067
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.801
Honestly, she said, "You know,
his heart's not what it was."

00:00:58.801 --> 00:01:01.200
[ Laughter ]
He lived for 20 years

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:03.100
[ Laughter ]
after that.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.100
I was freezing!

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.567
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:07.267
-Does it feel good to be
in front of an audience?

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.000
-It's so lovely to be back
in front of real people

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.267
for the first time
after two years.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.934
[ Cheering and applause ]
It's great.

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:14.567
It's great to be
in front of an audience.

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.267
It's great to be back
in the audience, you know,

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:17.801
going to shows again,
going to theater shows.

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:20.634
-I know you're a big music fan.
-Love going to live music.

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:23.200
The last time I went
to a concert was before COVID,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.901
was The Boss,
was Bruce Springsteen.

00:01:24.901 --> 00:01:26.300
I saw him on Broadway.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.100
I saw him in L.A.,
two shows in L.A.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.601
-Big Springsteen fan?
-Love The Boss,

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:32.167
absolutely adore the man.

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.501
The only problem is
I went to see Bruce

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:38.400
at The Forum in L.A. and I
very nearly broke his neck.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.801
[ Laughter ]
-Alright, explain this to me
now, okay.

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:42.701
-So I love The Boss
and I went down there.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.133
I went to show 1

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:46.067
and, during the show,
during "Hungry Heart,"

00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:48.734
he falls out into the crowd
and the crowd kind of carry him

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:50.467
on their hands.
-Oh, yeah, crowd surfing.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:51.601
-Crowd surfing, right?

00:01:51.601 --> 00:01:53.267
And so I'm loving it.
I'm watching this.

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:55.033
I was videoing it.
I did the whole thing.

00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:56.467
I just loved it.
-He's a god.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.634
-And I thought,
"I'm going to go back night 2

00:01:58.634 --> 00:01:59.734
because I want
a piece of that action.

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:01.467
I want to be one of those people

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:03.000
that gets to touch
the great man," right?

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.434
-So I go back.
I get a second ticket.

00:02:05.434 --> 00:02:07.300
I know where he's going to be,
you know, at what time.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.634
I know what he's going to do.

00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:11.133
I'm thinking, "I'm going
to touch this 70-year-old man!"

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.133
[ Laughter ]
You know? I'm like a stalker

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.167
in a retirement village.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.601
[ Laughter ]
Like I'm really --

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.167
I'm just waiting for him
to come out, right?

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:19.167
-Awful.
-So I get into position

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:20.567
and I'm waiting for him
and he comes out

00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:21.667
and he falls into the crowd.

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:23.100
♪ Everybody had a hungry heart ♪

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.167
and he's coming towards me
and I'm bracing myself for him.

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:27.200
And The Boss, he gets here
and I brace myself.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.834
And I receive The Boss.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:31.901
But I forget that I'm 6'7".

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.133
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.133 --> 00:02:36.701
So he starts going upwards,
right?

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:38.300
[ Laughter and applause ]
And I'm holding him,
and the person behind me

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.634
is like regular height
and they can't reach him.
-No.

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:42.934
[ Laughter ]
And so now I'm like --

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:45.701
There's a moment in time
where I'm taking the full weight

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.701
of Bruce Springsteen.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.634
I'm holding The Boss.

00:02:49.634 --> 00:02:51.634
And you know what?
Like he's a small man,

00:02:51.634 --> 00:02:53.234
but he's a lot heavier
than you'd think.

00:02:53.234 --> 00:02:54.601
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, it's a lot of -- Yes.

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:57.067
-Like, have you ever tried
to move like a sofa bed?

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.067
[ Laughter ]
You know? Like they're heavier
than they look, you know?

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:00.734
-He's a sturdy, sturdy man.

00:03:00.734 --> 00:03:02.801
-Well, he's dense with talent.
-He's dense with talent.

00:03:02.801 --> 00:03:04.567
[ Laughter ]
-And I'm holding like this.

00:03:04.567 --> 00:03:06.367
And I have a vision
of my grandmother saying,

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:08.000
"You should never hold
rock stars.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.801
[ Laughter and applause ]
You haven't got the

00:03:09.801 --> 00:03:10.934
upper body strength for that."
-You almost killed

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:12.434
Bruce Springsteen, yeah.

00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:13.634
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:03:13.634 --> 00:03:16.434
So, I just had to like bend
at the knees

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:19.467
and like finally hand him off
to the other person.

00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:21.100
And he was singing the whole
time. He didn't know it.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.567
-Saved his life.
-Saved his life.

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:25.067
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. Well, Bruce,
if you're watching,

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:27.133
that was Stephen who saved
your life at The Forum.

00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:29.467
Second night,
number 2, at The Forum.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:30.701
I want to talk
about "The Outlaws."

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:33.033
Congratulations on this.
-Thank you.

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:35.601
You write this.
It's a comedy-drama series,

00:03:35.601 --> 00:03:37.100
which I think you're great at,
too, because I think you're

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.534
also a very good,
dramatic actor.

00:03:39.534 --> 00:03:41.234
How did you come up
with this idea?

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:42.601
-My parents used to work,

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:44.267
when I was growing up,
for community service.

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.167
You know, if you get like a DUI
or some other crime,

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:47.968
they don't send you
to prison, necessarily.

00:03:47.968 --> 00:03:50.667
They make you pick up garbage
or, you know,

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.000
or renovate a building
as a punishment

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.434
and my parents were involved
with that when I was growing up.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:56.901
And I always thought
that was an interesting area

00:03:56.901 --> 00:03:58.434
for a comedy or a drama

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:01.734
because you'd have very unlikely
people sort of coming through.

00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:04.767
Like my mother, there was a kid
who I went to school with, Dave,

00:04:04.767 --> 00:04:07.234
who, he was always
coming through

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:09.667
and he was
the world's laziest thief.

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.901
[ Laughter ]
At one time, Dave got caught
in a house stealing the TV

00:04:12.901 --> 00:04:14.067
and the homeowners came in.

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:15.234
They're like, "Dave,
what are you doing?!"

00:04:15.234 --> 00:04:17.200
And he's like, "I'm not Dave."

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.567
And they're like,
"Yeah, you are!

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:19.767
You live next door!"

00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:21.767
[ Laughter ]
He didn't even go a block over.

00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:23.367
He was stealing
from his own neighbors.

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.234
[ Laughter ]
So there were people like that
come through

00:04:25.234 --> 00:04:26.767
and I always thought,
"What an interesting idea."

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:28.200
Like who are these people,
why are they doing

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.334
community service?
And let's make that into a show.

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.067
-And they have to come together.

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:34.868
And you have a great cast
and one of my favorites,

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.400
Christopher Walken,
is in this show.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.968
And, gosh, he's a treasure, man.

00:04:38.968 --> 00:04:40.634
-He's amazing.
He's brilliant guy.

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:42.701
Amazing in the show.
Fantastic.

00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:44.033
An honor to have him.
-He's one of the greatest.

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:46.567
And you got to film
in your hometown.

00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:48.767
-I filmed in my hometown
in the UK and we said to him,

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:51.234
"Is there anything you want
to do in England?"

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:53.501
He said, "I'd love
to go to Stonehenge."

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.000
So we went.
I took Christopher Walken

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.467
to Stonehenge.
-This is real.

00:04:56.467 --> 00:04:58.501
-This is the real thing.
-Talk about goals.

00:04:58.501 --> 00:05:00.434
-Right?
-Here's you

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:03.067
[ Laughter ]
and Christopher Walken
at Stonehenge.

00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:04.267
[ Awws and applause ]

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:05.534
That's amazing!
-Isn't that lovely?

00:05:05.534 --> 00:05:06.534
-Yeah, I love that.

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:10.234
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.667
Did he enjoy it?

00:05:11.667 --> 00:05:13.467
-And, again, he didn't say much
and then, at one point,

00:05:13.467 --> 00:05:15.634
like we'd been there for an hour
and he just said,

00:05:15.634 --> 00:05:19.400
Apparently, the blue stones
have healing properties."

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.534
[ Laughter ]
Okay. Wow.

00:05:21.534 --> 00:05:23.067
Is that how he would say that?
How would he say that?

00:05:23.067 --> 00:05:24.334
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:25.734
[ As Walken ]
And, apparently,

00:05:25.734 --> 00:05:27.801
the blue stones

00:05:27.801 --> 00:05:29.934
have healing properties.

00:05:29.934 --> 00:05:31.334
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:05:31.334 --> 00:05:34.834
[ Applause ]
[ Rimshot ]

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:38.200
So, he said, "May I touch
the stone?" to the guide.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.234
And it's such a British thing,
she said,

00:05:40.234 --> 00:05:41.200
"No."
No way.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.467
[ Laughter ]
I'm like, "Really?

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.501
You can't let Christopher Walken
touch one of the stones?

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:45.701
They've been here 5,000 years.

00:05:45.701 --> 00:05:47.000
-He's a legend, yeah.
-Come on. What do you do,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.834
take them in at night? Of course
people touch the stones."

00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:49.901
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, of course they do.
-They're not like

00:05:49.901 --> 00:05:51.734
lawn furniture.
You don't cover them up.

00:05:51.734 --> 00:05:54.767
[ Laughter ]
But she wouldn't let him.

00:05:54.767 --> 00:05:56.534
That's a bucket list thing

00:05:56.534 --> 00:05:57.834
that, probably,
you never thought would be

00:05:57.834 --> 00:05:59.534
-Oh, my goodness.
-in your bucket, yeah.

00:05:59.534 --> 00:06:01.033
You're like, "Wow,
I'm at Stonehenge

00:06:01.033 --> 00:06:02.400
with Christopher Walken.
Life is good."

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.133
-Really, that was
a surreal moment.

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:05.234
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.100
Here's Stephen Merchant
in "The Outlaws."

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.334
Take a look at this.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:10.901
-Greg, what's your secret?

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:11.801
-I'm a lawyer.

00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:14.300
-Keep that quiet.
Shameful.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:17.801
-A lawyer who's badly organized,
can't remember case precedents.

00:06:17.801 --> 00:06:21.033
I use Wikipedia to conduct most
of my legal research.

00:06:21.033 --> 00:06:23.467
I've missed court deadlines.
I've forged signatures.

00:06:23.467 --> 00:06:25.534
-We've all done that.
-In my office, I have
this cupboard.

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:26.934
I call it the cupboard of chaos.

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:29.968
I've stuffed every letter,
witness statement, affidavit,

00:06:29.968 --> 00:06:31.734
that's proof of my incompetence
in there

00:06:31.734 --> 00:06:33.234
and I can't destroy them,
I can't file them,

00:06:33.234 --> 00:06:36.200
so I live in constant fear that
someone will open the cupboard

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.167
and I'll never work
in law again,

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:39.400
even though I'm probably going
to get fired, anyway,

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.234
unless I bring in
some new clients, so.

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:44.067
Actually,
does anyone need a lawyer?

00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:46.400
[ Laughter ]
I've got some business
cards here.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.100
-Oh, yeah, yeah. Thank you.
Hey, are you any good?

00:06:51.968 --> 00:06:53.601
[ Laughter ]
-What do you think? Yeah.

00:06:53.601 --> 00:06:55.567
[ Cheering and applause ]
Stephen Merchant, everybody.

00:06:55.567 --> 00:06:57.133
Season 1 of "The Outlaws"

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:59.801
is available this Friday
on Prime Video.

